pub fn interleave_first_occurrences(left: &[String], right: &[String]) -> Vec<String>;
```

Visit values in interleaved order: `left[0]`, `right[0]`, `left[1]`, `right[1]`, and so on, skipping a side when its index is out of range. Emit a string only on its first occurrence anywhere in that traversal. Duplicate comparison is exact and case-sensitive. The relative order of retained strings must match their first appearances, and the inputs must not be modified.

Use only the Rust standard library. The function must be deterministic and must not use filesystem, network, clock, or random operations. Do not include a `main` function.

The current implementation suppresses only adjacent duplicates:

```rust
pub fn interleave_first_occurrences(left: &[String], right: &[String]) -> Vec<String> {
    let mut result = Vec::new();
    let mut last: Option<&str> = None;
    let count = left.len().max(right.len());

    for index in 0..count {
        if let Some(value) = left.get(index) {
            if last != Some(value.as_str()) {
                result.push(value.clone());
                last = Some(value.as_str());
            }
        }
        if let Some(value) = right.get(index) {
            if last != Some(value.as_str()) {
                result.push(value.clone());
                last = Some(value.as_str());
            }
        }
    }

    result
}
